---
intent: >
  Cross-cutting work for the brain config + update-playbook rework that does
  not live inside any single src/ module: rewrite the two shipped command
  files (`/aide:brain` and `/aide:update-playbook`) into thin routers, update
  the `brain-aide.md` methodology doc to describe the 6-section grammar,
  migrate this repo's own `.aide/config/brain.aide` to the new schema, and
  smoke-test the full rework end-to-end against the `aide-fresh` test repo
  at `D:\Code\Me\aide-fresh`.
---

## Project Structure

This plan is project-surface scoped — it only touches files outside `src/`.
The src/ work has its own four plans (parseBrainAide, tools/brain,
provisionBrain, cli/init). Every file below already exists; every change is
an in-place rewrite or migration. No new files are created by this plan.

`[E]` marks files modified in place by this plan. `[U]` marks files this
plan reads but does NOT modify (already updated upstream).

```
.aide/
├── intent.aide                                 [U] root spec — already rewritten by spec-writer + strategist
│                                                   to describe the 6-section schema; this plan reads but does NOT modify
├── plan.aide                                   [E] this file (overwrites prior 7/7 done plan)
├── config/
│   └── brain.aide                              [E] HOST SELF-UPDATE — migrate from 4-section legacy schema to 6-section
│                                                   rename `aide-prose-*` → `aide-orientation-*`
│                                                   rename `aide-playbook-*` → `aide-playbook-index-*`
│                                                   rename `aide-research-*` → `aide-research-index-*`
│                                                   keep `aide-study-playbook-*` unchanged
│                                                   insert NEW `aide-config-*` pair between orientation and playbook-index
│                                                   insert NEW `aide-update-playbook-*` pair between study-playbook and research-index
│                                                   final body order: orientation → config → playbook-index → study-playbook → update-playbook → research-index
│                                                   frontmatter unchanged (already on the new schema)
└── docs/
    └── brain-aide.md                           [E] METHODOLOGY DOC — rewrite to describe the 6-section grammar
                                                    update Format example, Body shape, Per-consumer ownership,
                                                    Lifecycle, Rules, Strict-failure migration paragraphs

.claude/commands/aide/
├── brain.md                                    [E] DIRECT EDIT (NOT pipelined per project CLAUDE.md)
│                                                   Mode 1 (general brain interaction) unchanged
│                                                   Mode 2 (config) becomes the new 5-step thin router:
│                                                     1. aide_info — branch on brain.status
│                                                     2. ensure brain.aide exists via aide_init({ category: "brain" }) with NO brainPath
│                                                     3. pull integration prose via aide_brain({ kind: "config" })
│                                                     4. execute returned instructions verbatim, forward $ARGUMENTS opaquely
│                                                     5. STOP
│                                                   Arguments docs reduce to: $ARGUMENTS is forwarded verbatim;
│                                                   per-backend shapes documented in brain.aide's config section
└── update-playbook.md                          [E] DIRECT EDIT — becomes 1-paragraph pointer mirroring study-playbook skill:
                                                    "Call aide_brain once. Following the brain access instructions it returns,
                                                    read coding-playbook/update-playbook.md and follow the playbook-maintenance
                                                    methodology it describes."
                                                    Existing checklist content is migrated to the brain via the new
                                                    updatePlaybook section seeded by provisionBrain (covered by other plan)
```

External-to-this-repo (read-only inspection during plan execution):

```
D:\Code\Me\aide-fresh\                          [U] smoke test target — read-only during plan, written to during smoke test step
├── .aide/config/brain.aide                         currently on legacy 3-section pre-pivot schema (aide-prose-* / aide-playbook-* / aide-research-*)
│                                                   smoke test will scaffold a fresh one via local dist after deletion
└── .mcp.json                                       points at D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/dist/index.js for aide MCP entry
                                                    smoke test will manually re-point or rely on existing pointer
```

## Prerequisites

Steps 4 and 5 in this plan have hard ordering dependencies on the four
module-level plans being landed first. Steps 1, 2, 3 are independent of src
and can run in parallel with the src plans.

- `src/service/parseBrainAide/` plan must execute first. Its `parseBrainAide`
  must export the 6-section grammar and return the typed shape
  `{ kind: "ok"; orientation; config; playbookIndex; studyPlaybook; updatePlaybook; researchIndex }`
  before this plan's Step 4 (host self-update verification) can run — Step 4
  verifies the migrated `.aide/config/brain.aide` parses as `kind: "ok"`
  against the new parser.

- `src/tools/brain/` plan must execute first. Its `aide_brain` tool must
  accept `kind?: "orientation" | "config"` and return the matching live
  section verbatim before Step 5 (smoke test) can verify
  `/aide:brain config` end-to-end.

- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/` plan must execute first. Its
  `provisionBrain` must seed `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` from the
  `updatePlaybook` typed key before Step 5 (smoke test) can verify
  `/aide:update-playbook` reads it from the brain.

- `src/cli/init/` plan must execute first. Its `init` command must accept an
  optional `brainPath` and land the `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder when absent
  before Step 5 (smoke test) can verify the cold-install no-`brainPath`
  flow.

- The default Obsidian template at
  `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts` must
  carry all 6 sections (rename + 2 new) before Step 5 (smoke test) can
  verify the scaffolded file has all 6.

## Plan

### 1. Rewrite `.claude/commands/aide/brain.md` Mode 2 to the 5-step thin router

Read: `coding-playbook/agentic/command-modules`,
`coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

This is a direct edit to the canonical shipped command file — per project
CLAUDE.md ("Don't run the pipeline on the agent harness"), `.claude/commands/`
files are edited directly, NOT through the AIDE pipeline.

Mode 1 (general brain interaction) stays exactly as it is. Only Mode 2
(config) is rewritten.

The new Mode 2 must:

- Forward `$ARGUMENTS` opaquely. The shipped command never parses
  `$ARGUMENTS` — never treats it as a path, a token, a JSON blob, or
  anything else. The integration's `config` body section is the only place
  that interprets the bytes.
- Never branch on backend identity. No `name`-sniffing, no
  `mcpServerConfig.args` introspection, no Obsidian-specific assumptions.
- Replace the existing 5-step Mode 2 (which embeds Obsidian-specific
  hint-driven path resolution and JSON-edit prose) with the new 5-step
  thin-router flow.

The new Mode 2 (config) flow, exactly:

- [x] **Step 1 — `aide_info`, branch on `brain.status`.**
  - `ok` → forward `$ARGUMENTS` and fall through to Step 3. The
    integration's prose decides what to do (typical case: re-point if
    `$ARGUMENTS` non-empty; STOP otherwise).
  - `no-brain-aide` → continue to Step 2.
  - `no-mcp-entry` / `mcp-drift` → skip Step 2, jump to Step 3.

- [x] **Step 2 — Ensure `brain.aide` exists.** Call
  `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` with NO `brainPath` argument. The
  default scaffold lands the `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder inside
  `mcpServerConfig.args`. After this, `brain.aide` exists on disk and
  `aide_brain` can be called.

- [x] **Step 3 — Pull integration-specific config prose.** Call
  `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })`. It returns the verbatim
  `<!-- aide-config-start -->` body section. Forward `$ARGUMENTS` opaquely
  — the shipped command does not parse it. The integration's prose alone
  interprets the bytes.

- [x] **Step 4 — Execute the returned instructions verbatim.** The
  integration's config prose owns: deciding what `$ARGUMENTS` means for
  this backend, asking the user for whatever else it needs (using
  `AskUserQuestion` plus `aide_info.brain.hints` when appropriate),
  editing `brain.aide` to land the resolved values, running sync via the
  Bash command recovered from `.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]`, and
  emitting the restart message.

- [x] **Step 5 — STOP.** Same anti-trap as today: do NOT re-call
  `aide_info` in the same session. The brain MCP server in the running
  session was launched without the new entry; an in-session re-check
  would report stale truth.

The `## Arguments` section must be rewritten to:

- [x] State that `$ARGUMENTS` is forwarded verbatim to the integration's
  config prose. The shipped command does not document any specific
  argument shape because there is none at the shipped layer. Each
  integration's `brain.aide` `config` section documents what users can put
  after `/aide:brain config` for that backend.

The existing `### Failure handling` section must be rewritten to:

- [x] Cover only the Step 2 `aide_init` failure case (scaffold failed).
  Sync failure is handled inside Step 4's verbatim instructions, since the
  integration's prose now owns the sync invocation.

Existing Obsidian-specific content (the hint-driven path-resolution
question, the inline absolute-path prompt, the JSON edit details, the
specific sync recovery command examples, the verbatim post-sync message
text) must NOT be preserved in the shipped command. That content is moving
into the default Obsidian `config` body section, which is added by the
`provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate` plan.

### 2. Rewrite `.claude/commands/aide/update-playbook.md` to the one-paragraph pointer

Read: `coding-playbook/agentic/command-modules`

Direct edit. The new file mirrors `study-playbook` skill shape exactly: one
short paragraph instructing the agent to call `aide_brain` once, then read
the maintenance methodology from the brain.

- [x] Replace the entire body (frontmatter / agent annotation / checklist /
  housekeeping step / drift-detection prose) with this prose, framed
  appropriately for a `.claude/commands/aide/` file (keep the H1 title
  line, drop the agent annotation since this is no longer an
  orchestrator-with-checklist):

  > Call `aide_brain` once. Following the brain access instructions it
  > returns, read `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` and follow the
  > playbook-maintenance methodology it describes.

- [x] Remove the `> **Agent:** You are the orchestrator for this command. Do
  NOT delegate to a subagent.` annotation — it does not apply to a
  thin-pointer command.

- [x] The H1 title `# /aide:update-playbook — Playbook Maintenance` stays.

- [x] The existing checklist content (ask the user what they want to
  change; identify the relevant section; apply the change; offer
  reorganization; routing-table drift check; apply changes; confirm final
  state) is NOT preserved in this command file. That content moves into the
  default Obsidian `update-playbook` brain.aide body section, seeded by
  `provisionBrain` at install time. The seeded brain file at
  `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` is the live source after install.

### 3. Update `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` to describe the 6-section grammar

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

This is a content/documentation rewrite of the methodology doc. Every
agent across every host project reads this doc through `aide_init`'s
methodology-doc copy step, so accuracy here is reputation.

The doc must end up describing exactly what the 6-section grammar IS and
how it works — not how it differs from prior schemas (drop the historical
sub-narrative; the strategist already captured the migration story in the
intent spec's Context).

- [x] **Update the `## Format` YAML+body example.** Replace the 4-section
  body example with a 6-section example showing all six marker pairs in
  required order: `<!-- aide-orientation-* -->`, `<!-- aide-config-* -->`,
  `<!-- aide-playbook-index-* -->`, `<!-- aide-study-playbook-* -->`,
  `<!-- aide-update-playbook-* -->`, `<!-- aide-research-index-* -->`.
  Inside each, write a one-sentence placeholder describing what content
  belongs there. The `mcpServerConfig.args` example may show the
  `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder shape used by the new no-`brainPath` cold
  install.

- [x] **Update the `### Body shape` paragraph.** State that the body is SIX
  marker-bounded sections in fixed order. List the 12 markers (six
  open/close pairs) in required order. Reaffirm the marker grammar: paired
  open/close, lowercase, single ASCII spaces around the token,
  case-sensitive.

- [x] **Add the live-vs-seed split as a new subsection under
  `### Body shape` or as a new top-level paragraph.** Sections 1-2
  (`orientation`, `config`) are live — read by `aide_brain` at runtime.
  Sections 3-6 (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
  `researchIndex`) are install-time seeds — written into the brain on cold
  install, then owned by the user there. The `aide_brain` tool never
  reads seed sections; install service never reads live sections.

- [x] **Update the `**Per-consumer section ownership.**` block** to list
  six entries instead of four:
  - `orientation` — owned by `aide_brain` (live; `kind: "orientation"`).
    Returns verbatim to the agent.
  - `config` — owned by `aide_brain` (live; `kind: "config"`). Returns
    verbatim to the integration-specific wiring flow that
    `/aide:brain config` executes.
  - `playbookIndex` — owned by `provisionBrain`. Read once, written as
    `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`.
  - `studyPlaybook` — owned by `provisionBrain`. Read once, written as
    `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`.
  - `updatePlaybook` — owned by `provisionBrain`. Read once, written as
    `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`. NEW.
  - `researchIndex` — owned by `provisionBrain`. Read once, written as
    `research/research.md`.

- [x] **Update the `**Strict-failure migration policy.**` paragraph** to
  cover the new migration class. Pre-rework files (4 sections with
  `aide-prose-*` / `aide-playbook-*` / `aide-study-playbook-*` /
  `aide-research-*` markers) return `malformed-body` naming all expected
  new markers as missing. No transitional read path, no auto-injection,
  no rename rewrite. Hosts hand-edit. Mention the prior migration classes
  briefly only to anchor the strict-failure pattern; do not narrate the
  full schema-evolution history.

- [x] **Update `## Substitution surface`** to list all six body sections
  by name. The substitution surface is `mcpServerConfig.args` only;
  body sections are returned verbatim across all six.

- [x] **Update `## Lifecycle` step 1** to describe a 6-section scaffold:
  the canonical Obsidian default carries `name: obsidian`, an
  `mcpServerConfig` with the `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder when no
  `brainPath` was supplied (or the path inline when supplied via
  `init --brain-path`), and six pre-filled body sections.

- [x] **Update `## Rules`** so the rule line that currently reads "All four
  body sections returned verbatim" now reads "All six body sections
  returned verbatim", and the rule line about per-consumer ownership lists
  all six sections + their owners. Add a new rule covering the live-vs-seed
  split: `aide_brain` reads only the live pair (`orientation`, `config`);
  install service reads only the seed quartet (`playbookIndex`,
  `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`).

- [x] **Update `## Boot reporter brain status states`** — no change to
  the four states (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`),
  but the `no-brain-aide` remediation prose may reference the new
  `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder behavior if accuracy demands it.

- [x] **Add a new short section: `## aide_brain kind parameter`.**
  Documents that `aide_brain` accepts `kind?: "orientation" | "config"`
  defaulting to `"orientation"`, and explicitly states that seed-section
  kinds (`playbook-index`, `study-playbook`, `update-playbook`,
  `research-index`) are NOT exposed at runtime — after install, those
  sections become on-disk artifacts in the brain that the agent reads via
  the brain's own MCP read tool.

- [x] **Drop or update the `## Divergence from intent specs`** content
  only if it now refers to specifics that changed. Most of it (no
  `scope`, no `outcomes`, etc.) is unchanged.

- [x] **Update `## Placement`** if needed — the directory tree showing
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` is unchanged.

### 4. Migrate this repo's `.aide/config/brain.aide` to the new 6-section schema

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

**Ordering: this step REQUIRES the `parseBrainAide` plan to be landed
first.** Verification at the end of this step depends on the parser
returning `kind: "ok"` for the migrated file under the new grammar. If
this step runs before the parser change is built and tests pass, the
verification will fail with `malformed-body` (which is itself the correct
strict-failure outcome — but the step's success criterion is `kind: "ok"`).

The current `.aide/config/brain.aide` has:

- Frontmatter on the new schema (`name: obsidian`, `mcpServerConfig` with
  `command: cmd` and the cmd-wrapped `npx @bitbonsai/mcpvault` args
  pointing at `D:\Code\Me\my-brain`). UNCHANGED.
- Body with 4 legacy sections: `aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`,
  `aide-study-playbook-*`, `aide-research-*`. MIGRATE.

The migration is exactly what the spec's strict-failure migration policy
describes: a hand-edit, not a code-driven rewrite.

