- [ ] **5i. Surface any drift, regressions, or unexpected behavior.**
  If any verification fails, capture the exact failure mode (which
  step, what was expected, what happened) and surface it back to the
  user as a smoke-test report. The user decides whether to roll back
  or patch and re-test. Do NOT attempt automatic rollback — this is
  user-supervised verification, not CI.

## Decisions

1. **Why this is a single project-surface plan instead of being absorbed
   into the four module plans.** The module plans are scoped to their
   respective `src/` folders and the progressive-disclosure single-module
   scoping rule forbids them from describing files outside their scope.
   The four cross-cutting surfaces (two shipped commands, the methodology
   doc, the host self-update, and the smoke test) sit at the project
   surface and have no natural home in any single src module. The root
   `.aide/plan.aide` is exactly where project-surface work belongs.

2. **Why `.claude/commands/` files get direct edits, NOT pipelined
   sub-plans.** Project CLAUDE.md is explicit: "Don't run the pipeline
   on the agent harness." The `.claude/commands/` files ARE the runtime
   of the AIDE pipeline; running `/aide:spec → research → plan → build`
   on them is a circular dependency. Direct edit by a fresh
   implementor agent is the right shape.

3. **Why the host self-update (Step 4) and the smoke test (Step 5) come
   after the four src plans.** Step 4 verifies the migrated file parses
   as `kind: "ok"` against the new parser — that requires the new parser
   to exist. Step 5 exercises the entire rework end-to-end through
   `aide_init`, `aide_brain`, the new shipped command files, and
   `provisionBrain`'s seed step — that requires every src plan to be
   landed AND the two shipped commands (Steps 1 and 2) to be rewritten.
   The Prerequisites section codifies this dependency explicitly so the
   orchestrator does not run Steps 4 or 5 prematurely.

4. **Why the smoke test is end-to-end and manual rather than automated.**
   Per the user's instruction quoted in the delegation: "build, run the
   installer directly from the dist on disk, manually update the
   .mcp.json file in the other app to point to our local dist, then run
   claude in that project and test the rest." The smoke test exercises
   the MCP boundary (between Claude Code's process and the local dist),
   the slash-command surface (which has no Vitest coverage by design),
   and the integration-prose execution path (which depends on the
   model's runtime behavior). Automating any of these would mock out
   exactly the surfaces under test. The smoke test is human-in-the-loop
   verification, not unit testing — and that is the right shape for a
   shipped-command rework.

5. **Why `brain.md` Mode 1 stays untouched and only Mode 2 changes.**
   Mode 1 is the general brain-interaction path that has no integration
   coupling — it calls `aide_brain` with no arguments (the default
   `kind: "orientation"` returns the live orientation section), reads
   the entry-point file the orientation section names, and fulfills the
   user's request through the brain's MCP tools. Nothing in Mode 1
   parses `$ARGUMENTS` or branches on backend identity. Mode 1 is
   already the shape Mode 2 is being rewritten into; the rework only
   touches the part that wasn't yet a thin router.

6. **Why `update-playbook.md` keeps its filename and H1 even though it's
   no longer a checklist.** The slash command surface
   `/aide:update-playbook` is documented externally and can't be
   renamed without breaking host expectations. The H1 stays because
   slash-command files conventionally name themselves in their first
   line. The new content is one paragraph, the rest of the file is just
   framing.

7. **Why the `update-playbook.md` agent annotation
   (`> **Agent:** You are the orchestrator for this command. Do NOT
   delegate to a subagent.`) is removed.** That annotation existed
   because the legacy command was a multi-step orchestrator. The new
   command is a one-paragraph pointer — there is no orchestration to
   delegate. Keeping the annotation would lie about the command's
   shape and tempt agents into following pipeline conventions on a
   non-pipeline command.

8. **Why `brain-aide.md` drops the schema-evolution narrative and
   only describes the current 6-section grammar.** The methodology
   doc's job is to describe what `brain.aide` IS, not how it got
   here. The strategist's intent.aide already captures the 3 → 4 → 6
   schema-evolution story in `## Context` for future architects who
   need to understand why the schema looks this way. The methodology
   doc should be a clean reference for users authoring or migrating a
   `brain.aide`, not a history.

9. **Why the host self-update step (4) is bundled into this plan
   rather than executed manually after build.** It's part of the
   rework's definition of done — until this repo's own
   `.aide/config/brain.aide` parses as `kind: "ok"` against the new
   schema, the rework hasn't fully landed in its own home. Bundling
   it as Step 4 ensures the orchestrator doesn't accidentally ship a
   release where the canonical home of AIDE has a malformed brain
   config. The verification sub-step (run parser tests / aide_validate)
   makes the success criterion explicit.

10. **Why Step 5 (smoke test) is a single numbered step with many
    lettered sub-steps instead of being split.** The smoke test is a
    sequential session in a single test repo with shared in-flight
    state (built dist on disk, MCP server registration, Claude Code
    session). Sub-steps 5a-5i must run in order in the same session;
    splitting them across multiple agents would lose continuity (e.g.,
    verifying that step 5f's edit landed correctly in step 5g requires
    knowing what 5f did). One agent runs the whole smoke test; the
    lettered sub-steps document the verification points so the agent
    knows what to check at each gate.
